WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.490
The Sea of Japan illustrates historical back-arc opening.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.970
Japan's rocks once belonged to an arc at the Asian margin.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.690
Rifting and seafloor spreading separated that arc from the mainland.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.210
That history does not, by itself, establish that spreading is active

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.850
there today. The neighboring shallow China Sea instead largely overlies continental

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:24.070
crust. Similar-looking water bodies need not have the same crust or

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:24.530
origin.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.490
The Philippine Sea contains several basins separated by submarine ridges.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.490
Near the Marianas, the source describes an active back-arc basin, while

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:37.010
older ridges farther west are pieces of arc separated during earlier

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.690
episodes. The Mariana trench lies on the incoming-plate side of the

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:44.570
arc. Each abandoned ridge and intervening basin records a different part

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.650
of that migrating system.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.110
The Java Sea and shallow seas between Australia and Papua New

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:54.870
Guinea occupy continental platforms. They are not all newly created oceanic

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.570
basins. Farther east, small plates, trenches, arcs and basins make the

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:05.270
region unusually complex. Tonga provides another clear subduction-and-back-arc example.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.050
Read the crustal setting and boundaries before choosing a mechanism from

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:09.750
the sea's name.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.870
The Bering Sea demonstrates a different history.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.050
Its eastern part is shallow above continental crust;

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.830
its western basin is deeper above oceanic crust.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.150
The source reconstructs an older subduction zone near the mainland followed

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.790
by a shift offshore to the Aleutian system.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.590
Oceanic crust became trapped behind the new arc.

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.810
The mainland edge can be passive even though the arc offshore

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:33.510
is active.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:37.950
The Gulf of California formed as the plate boundary changed and

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.950
Baja separated from mainland Mexico.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.970
Baja now moves northward relative to North America as part of

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.830
the Pacific plate. Strike-slip faults link short spreading segments in the

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.330
gulf. This combines sideways displacement and local spreading.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.570
Japan's back-arc opening and the Bering Sea's trapped crust have different

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:55.950
histories.
